{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation","id":1192205,"title":"Specialties after character generation","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-03-06T05:10:00-08:00","id":1192205,"title":"Specialties after character generation","content":"<span style=\"font-family:inherit\"> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">Is it too late to make it so that getting 3 in a skill, both before and after character generation, gives you a specialty? I assumed this was the case until one of the players in a Trinity game, who's wary of chargen optimization traps, noticed it and started to make sure that all his skills were at 3 so he didn't start with a deficit of potential XP. I checked to see whether it worked this way in Scion, and after an initially misleading sentence at the bottom of page 60, page 105 confirmed it was the same. It could be houseruled fairly easily, but I think having the canon rules set up to create XP imbalances at character generation is an easily solved problem, either by removing free character generation specialties, or by saying that everyone picks one up at the third dot of a skill.</span><br /> </span><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23646-goblinsociety","name":"GoblinSociety","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-06T11:58:59-08:00","id":1192338,"title":"","content":"Given that Scion uses flat XP costs, it's probably not the biggest trap. But it sounds like an easy enough house rule to make.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T01:42:43-08:00","id":1192555,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GoblinSociety</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation#post1192205\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\"> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">Is it too late to make it so that getting 3 in a skill, both before and after character generation, gives you a specialty? I assumed this was the case until one of the players in a Trinity game, who's wary of chargen optimization traps, noticed it and started to make sure that all his skills were at 3 so he didn't start with a deficit of potential XP. I checked to see whether it worked this way in Scion, and after an initially misleading sentence at the bottom of page 60, page 105 confirmed it was the same. It could be houseruled fairly easily, but I think having the canon rules set up to create XP imbalances at character generation is an easily solved problem, either by removing free character generation specialties, or by saying that everyone picks one up at the third dot of a skill.</span><br /> </span><br /></div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, such a character would also start with fewer skills in general, which is never a good thing. I think you can get at most, what, seven skills to three dots at character creation? With a minimum of three? And since specialties cost three compared to a skill's five that's a net gain of eight xp? I think that's maybe only 2-4 sessions worth.. And since specialties are mostly useful for the extra momentum, a group resource in most games, it's not like they are outshining the other players with it. It doesn't seem like a huge problem in general, and as was pointed out, easy enough to house rule if it does come up","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8375-vonpenguin","name":"vonpenguin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T02:02:03-08:00","id":1192557,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vonpenguin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1192555#post1192555\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> To be fair, such a character would also start with fewer skills in general, which is never a good thing. I think you can get at most, what, seven skills to three dots at character creation? With a minimum of three? And since specialties cost three compared to a skill's five that's a net gain of eight xp? I think that's maybe only 2-4 sessions worth.. And since specialties are mostly useful for the extra momentum, a group resource in most games, it's not like they are outshining the other players with it. It doesn't seem like a huge problem in general, and as was pointed out, easy enough to house rule if it does come up</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, Specialties also give you +1 Enhancement to areas the specialty applies to outside of the base skill. The example the book uses is having a Firearms specialty in Pistols, and getting 1E on a Persuasion roll to convince some mercenaries to use a specific brand of pistols.<br /> <br /> But yeah, specialties aren't really required to get in boatloads to make your Objectively Optimal One True Build.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T03:11:18-08:00","id":1192564,"title":"","content":"+1 Enhancement is really strong. It's the equivalent of 3 extra dice, if you already succeed at something. The only downside is that they can't help you get that single success. Fortunately, that's where Momentum comes in, allowing you to add extra dice, allowing you to more reliably succeed. A character with enough specialties is basically always going to be getting an extra Enhancement on their roll, and that's really powerful.<br /> <br /> And 7 skills is not actually bad - Most people can justify dropping a chunk of them as not relevant to their character concept. The bigger loss, IMO, is not having a few skills at 5.<br /> <br /> The biggest problem though is that it seems to run against the design philosophy of Storypath. Previous versions of these games have been filled with hideous optimisation traps. Buying certain things at certain points was objectively better. It heavily encouraged min-maxing character gen, because the character who did was better right out of the gate, and was able to easily broaden their focus to be just as good as the generalist in a few sessions.<br /> <br /> Storypath kills that dead. All levels of an attribute cost the same, whether the first or fifth. Same for abilities. There's nothing that's really efficient to buy at character creation, but inefficient later. Hell, the entire tweak system means that you can completely re-design your character's skills if you find that it's not doing what you want. Except that breaks down with skill specialties. A character with maxed specialties has 12XP (3xp per specialty, 4 extra specialties) that other characters don't. They are, no matter what else happens, objectively better off. And that will continue throughout the game - They have fewer skills at 5, but it doesn't take them long to fix that. They have fewer one dot skills, but how often do you need every member of a band to Pilot, or perform Medicine?<br /> <br /> Also, it's mechanically messy. How does it interact with Tweaks? If I re-focus out of one skill and into another, what happens to my specialty? As best I can tell, they keep it (You only need 3 dice to gain a specialty, not to use it), even if they drop the skill down to 0 (Which isn't unreasonable - Over a sufficiently long campaign, you could absolutely justify losing skills entirely. Scions can easily go through multiple, radical changes. Even without that, a lot of skills will drop below 3. If I get a magic bow, I'm probably going to stop practising with my guns. If I go blind, I'm probably not going to be practising my piloting). There's no way to transfer it to the skill that you actually use, and for some reason you keep getting Enhancements related to your now defunct specialty.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2649,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2649-tarion","user":"Tarion","date":". 03-07-2018, 06:13 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2649-tarion","name":"Tarion","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"85"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T08:41:55-08:00","id":1192618,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyman201</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1192557#post1192557\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> To be fair, Specialties also give you +1 Enhancement to areas the specialty applies to outside of the base skill. The example the book uses is having a Firearms specialty in Pistols, and getting 1E on a Persuasion roll to convince some mercenaries to use a specific brand of pistols.<br /> <br /> But yeah, specialties aren't really required to get in boatloads to make your Objectively Optimal One True Build.</div> </div> </div> </div>I know, but that strikes me as a secondary bonus, and if my combat monkey doesn't have even a single point in social what are the odds the enhancement will come into play? Like I said, it's pretty trivial to houserule and the price to get them latter isn't really prohibitive.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8375-vonpenguin","name":"vonpenguin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T09:13:41-08:00","id":1192631,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vonpenguin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1192618#post1192618\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I know, but that strikes me as a secondary bonus, and if my combat monkey doesn't have even a single point in social what are the odds the enhancement will come into play? Like I said, it's pretty trivial to houserule and the price to get them latter isn't really prohibitive.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Something being trivial to fix on our own, and not that bad when it's not fixed, doesn't make it something that shouldn't be fixed by the people who made the book while they can still do it. There's absolutely no reason I can imagine for this to work as it does right now, and several good reasons it shouldn't. It's not about powergaming, it's about the fact that someone can start with up to the equivalent of 14 more points of XP than someone else entirely by accident, because they wanted to be pretty good at a bunch of things, and the other guy wanted more focus or a more diverse spread. That's just bad.<br /> <br /> There's no argument for it, and 'you can fix this bad thing on your own' is true, but between the choice of something with known flaws that can be easily fixed and something without those flaws, it's pretty clear which is better.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23646,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23646-goblinsociety","user":"GoblinSociety","date":". 03-07-2018, 12:18 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23646-goblinsociety","name":"GoblinSociety","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T09:25:01-08:00","id":1192639,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GoblinSociety</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1192631#post1192631\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Something being trivial to fix on our own, and not that bad when it's not fixed, doesn't make it something that shouldn't be fixed by the people who made the book while they can still do it. There's absolutely no reason I can imagine for this to work as it does right now, and several good reasons it shouldn't. It's not about powergaming, it's about the fact that someone can start with up to the equivalent of 14 more points of XP than someone else entirely by accident, because they wanted to be pretty good at a bunch of things, and the other guy wanted more focus or a more diverse spread. That's just bad.<br /> <br /> There's no argument for it, and 'you can fix this bad thing on your own' is true, but between the choice of something with known flaws that can be easily fixed and something without those flaws, it's pretty clear which is better.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you're <b>greatly</b> overstating how big an impact some extra specialties will be. The specialties aren't really something you NEED.<br /> <br /> They generate momentum, and give a bonus success on things that aren't that main area of focus. A PC who starts with four specialties isn't going to be markedly more effective than a PC who starts with three specialties. They'll just provide a bit more Momentum. That's hardly game-shaking<br /> <br /> I mean, if it bothers you and your players that much then by all means, add the house rule, but I <b>really</b> don't think it's something in desperate need of patching because it's Bad Design.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T11:45:31-08:00","id":1192688,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyman201</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1192639#post1192639\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you're <b>greatly</b> overstating how big an impact some extra specialties will be. The specialties aren't really something you NEED.<br /> <br /> They generate momentum, and give a bonus success on things that aren't that main area of focus. A PC who starts with four specialties isn't going to be markedly more effective than a PC who starts with three specialties. They'll just provide a bit more Momentum. That's hardly game-shaking<br /> <br /> I mean, if it bothers you and your players that much then by all means, add the house rule, but I <b>really</b> don't think it's something in desperate need of patching because it's Bad Design.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'm stating exactly what it is, which is a potential difference of 14 XP effectively spent between a player that picks skills with this in mind and one who doesn't. I can't see any reason to defend that. A small problem is still a problem. Can you tell me any upsides to this being in the book that I'm missing?<br /> <br /> Edit: I checked the XP tables after posting this, because the only thing worse than being pedantic is being pedantic and wrong. A specialty is actually 3 XP, which results in a potential difference of 21 XP between someone who spends their points in a way that results in no free specialties at character generation, and someone who spends to maximize the number of specialties. Assuming players earn 3-5 XP a session, this is anywhere between a month and almost two months of play, and over 4 skill dots, two knacks or two attribute points of difference.<br /> <br /> If you think that this is a good thing to be in the system, let me know why! Personally, I don't think the ability for people to make mistakes that put themselves behind other players at character generation or make choices to put themselves ahead at the same time is a good idea, and if it can be trivially fixed, then I hope the developers have the time to trivially fix it instead of the fans doing it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23646,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23646-goblinsociety","user":"GoblinSociety","date":". 03-07-2018, 03:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23646-goblinsociety","name":"GoblinSociety","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T13:58:16-08:00","id":1192752,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GoblinSociety</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1192688#post1192688\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I'm stating exactly what it is, which is a potential difference of 14 XP effectively spent between a player that picks skills with this in mind and one who doesn't. I can't see any reason to defend that. A small problem is still a problem. Can you tell me any upsides to this being in the book that I'm missing?<br /> <br /> Edit: I checked the XP tables after posting this, because the only thing worse than being pedantic is being pedantic and wrong. A specialty is actually 3 XP, which results in a potential difference of 21 XP between someone who spends their points in a way that results in no free specialties at character generation, and someone who spends to maximize the number of specialties. Assuming players earn 3-5 XP a session, this is anywhere between a month and almost two months of play, and over 4 skill dots, two knacks or two attribute points of difference.<br /> <br /> If you think that this is a good thing to be in the system, let me know why! Personally, I don't think the ability for people to make mistakes that put themselves behind other players at character generation or make choices to put themselves ahead at the same time is a good idea, and if it can be trivially fixed, then I hope the developers have the time to trivially fix it instead of the fans doing it.</div> </div> </div> </div>See, your mistaken assumption is that Specialties are something people will want and immediately get in everything. They're nice, but not required by any means. <br /> <br /> Honestly, the main difference between someone who minmaxes to get specialties in everything and someone who doesn't is that the minmaxer will have bigger dice pools than the generalist in certain areas.<br /> <br /> Like, some people won't use Specialties or want to buy them after chargen. You don't need a specialty in each ability to have a character function","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T14:15:53-08:00","id":1192761,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyman201</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1192752#post1192752\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> See, your mistaken assumption is that Specialties are something people will want and immediately get in everything. They're nice, but not required by any means.<br /> <br /> Honestly, the main difference between someone who minmaxes to get specialties in everything and someone who doesn't is that the minmaxer will have bigger dice pools than the generalist in certain areas.<br /> <br /> Like, some people won't use Specialties or want to buy them after chargen. You don't need a specialty in each ability to have a character function</div> </div> </div> </div>Meanwhile, your mistaken assumption is that whether people will want to get them is at all relevant. You're ignoring the fact that this results in different XP totals at character generation without any necessary intent to do that on the player's behalf, and you haven't been able to state why this is a feature and not a bug. The fact that this is a specialty is entirely immaterial. The fact that this is a potential difference of 21 XP is the matter at hand.<br /> <br /> Your posts have been fixated on the idea of the specialty, rather than the idea of the XP disparity. If people could get different amounts of XP at character generation through spending bonus points that don't match up to later XP costs, as they can in third edition Exalted, or if people could roll a 22-sided die and get between 0 and 21 XP, that would still be a problem. If you think that XP disparities at character generation is a feature and not a bug, explain why. If you agree with me that this isn't desirable, I'm not sure why you're arguing with me. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23646-goblinsociety","name":"GoblinSociety","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T14:17:28-08:00","id":1192764,"title":"","content":"The idea that a GM can edit the rules they don't like out of the game does not excuse the writer of an RPG to provide the best, most balanced product they can. Since it seems pretty objectively wrong to create such a huge difference in XP effectively spent at character creation, I really don't see why it should be left as it is.<br /> <br /> Honestly, Kyman, you really should answer OP's question: what's the advantage of leaving it as it is? Because I sure can't see it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23975-pxgentleman","name":"PXGentleman","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"26"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T15:04:25-08:00","id":1192792,"title":"","content":"It's less a thing about the advantage of leaving it in and more making a mountain out of not even a molehill, but a small sign that says &quot;There may be moles here&quot;<br /> <br /> I honestly don't think making the change will change much, if at all. Again, the mistaken assumption of XP disparity seems to hinge on the assumption that a player will, naturally, move to acquire specialties to equal the number of specialties acquired by another player.<br /> <br /> This seems to be a mistaken assumption. If the player has fewer specialties than another, there's not going to be a major need for them to acquire more. From what I see of the rules, there's not a gigantic mechanical edge over having a specialty and not having one. More momentum is nice, and not being totally helpless with creative applications of the specialty is nice, but neither will make or break the experience.<br /> <br /> And you noting that the amount of experience needed to 'catch up' to someone who maxed their Specialties could be spend on Skills or Attributes or Knacks... Frankly? This assumes that you would buy a specialty over a Knack or more dots.<br /> <br /> I honestly don't think 99% of players would.<br /> <br /> Like, yeah, XP disparity is a problem, especially in say Exalted where allocation of bonus points and stay dots can make 20+ needed experience to catch up.<br /> <br /> But this seems more like a 'Principle of the thing' rather than a problem that will actually impact the majority of players. Worth bringing up? Sure. A huge problem the OP seems to think it is? I wouldn't go that far.<br /> <br /> Hell, the proposed rule change has ways to exploit it. Don't forget, you can shift skill dots around between sessions in Scion. If say, Jim has 1 dot in Firearms at Chargen and 2 in Integrity, then after Session 1 he could swap the Firearms dot to Integrity. Would that give Jim a free Specialty?<br /> <br /> What if after Session 2, Jim moves an Integrity dot to Close Combat? Would he LOSE the Integrity Specialty?<br /> <br /> Edit: And another thing... This minmaxer, let's call him Frank. What if, to get more Specialties, he started with no dots in Integrity because who needs Integrity? He then gets an answer when the party runs afoul with some Sirens, and wants to buy up Integrity.<br /> <br /> Jim, who DID put a dot in Integrity at Chargen, buys his up to three. Skills are what, 4 xp a dot? I don't have the book in front of me.<br /> <br /> Either way, Jim spent less experience on Integrity after Chargen, because Frank wanted a Subterfuge Specialty. Hell, Jim could take the XP he saved in not buying another dot in Integrity and buy a Specialty if he wants, potentially in Integrity.<br /> <br /> Is that a disparity that needs addressing? Or does it all kind of settle?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":2142,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","user":"Kyman201","date":". 03-07-2018, 06:42 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T15:49:06-08:00","id":1192826,"title":"","content":"So if you don't think it's an advantage to leave in, and you see it as an issue that could cause problems, I'm glad we agree that it should be fixed! My proposals weren't intended to be ironclad. I, after all, am not a writer on Scion.<br /> <br /> However, now that we're united in seeing it as an issue (not the major, game-ruining one you claim I believe it is, but an issue worth mentioning on an internet forum, in the hopes that it can be fixed before the text is finalized), we can end this argument and return to the original point: It's a small oversight, and now is the time to fix small oversights.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23646,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23646-goblinsociety","user":"GoblinSociety","date":". 03-07-2018, 06:54 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23646-goblinsociety","name":"GoblinSociety","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T16:24:35-08:00","id":1192840,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>GoblinSociety</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1192826#post1192826\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So if you don't think it's an advantage to leave in, and you see it as an issue that could cause problems, I'm glad we agree that it should be fixed! My proposals weren't intended to be ironclad. I, after all, am not a writer on Scion.<br /> <br /> However, now that we're united in seeing it as an issue (not the major, game-ruining one you claim I believe it is, but an issue worth mentioning on an internet forum, in the hopes that it can be fixed before the text is finalized), we can end this argument and return to the original point: It's a small oversight, and now is the time to fix small oversights.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually the more I think on my thing about drifting a dot around between sessions to cheese for free specialties, the more I think it's best to leave it as-is.<br /> <br /> Edit: I mean, not to say that you shouldn't have brought it up. These things should be discussed. I just think if you're going to put in a houserule, the reading should, at least, be &quot;When you spend experience to raise a Skill to 3 dots, you gain a free Specialty&quot; or something.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T16:44:33-08:00","id":1192851,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyman201</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1192840#post1192840\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Actually the more I think on my thing about drifting a dot around between sessions to cheese for free specialties, the more I think it's best to leave it as-is.<br /> <br /> Edit: I mean, not to say that you shouldn't have brought it up. These things should be discussed. I just think if you're going to put in a houserule, the reading should, at least, be &quot;When you spend experience to raise a Skill to 3 dots, you gain a free Specialty&quot; or something.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Oh, yeah, if I have to houserule it, that was my intent. I'd proposed that without thinking of the ability to move stats between sessions.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23646-goblinsociety","name":"GoblinSociety","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-07T17:24:16-08:00","id":1192860,"title":"","content":"Hey, sorry I got heated. I didn't intend it, but rereading I can see how they came across more aggressive than intended","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-08T00:56:05-08:00","id":1193008,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyman201</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1192840#post1192840\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Actually the more I think on my thing about drifting a dot around between sessions to cheese for free specialties, the more I think it's best to leave it as-is.<br /> <br /> Edit: I mean, not to say that you shouldn't have brought it up. These things should be discussed. I just think if you're going to put in a houserule, the reading should, at least, be &quot;When you spend experience to raise a Skill to 3 dots, you gain a free Specialty&quot; or something.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd go the other way entirely - If you're still giving benefits to purchasing things in the right order, you're not actually solving the problem. <br /> <br /> If you instead phrase it as &quot;Any Skill at rating 3 or higher has a Specialty&quot;, you solve all the issues. It removes order of operations character creation entirely. You can freely change your character over the course of a campaign without losing out on any experience. <br /> <br /> I've been looking into this as a general Storypath thing and it looks like specialties are probably more valuable in Trinity Continuum, where they gate a whole load of things. If you don't have a relevant specialty you can't hack computers, craft anything, or pilot unusual vehicles. Lots of people are going to need to be picking up specialties as their circumstances change. I wouldn't be surprised if Scion picks up a few of those things, because Trinity has tended to act as the master document.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2649-tarion","name":"Tarion","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"85"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-08T09:21:43-08:00","id":1193165,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tarion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1193008#post1193008\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've been looking into this as a general Storypath thing and it looks like specialties are probably more valuable in Trinity Continuum, where they gate a whole load of things. If you don't have a relevant specialty you can't hack computers, craft anything, or pilot unusual vehicles. Lots of people are going to need to be picking up specialties as their circumstances change. I wouldn't be surprised if Scion picks up a few of those things, because Trinity has tended to act as the master document.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'd be surprised if they did that, honestly. It seems to run contrary to what they're trying to do with Scion. Trinity inspired a lot of Scion, yes, but Neall's been putting his own spin on things.<br /> <br /> Like the lack of Skill Tricks. Trinity has Skill Tricks, but in Scion there's <i>Knacks</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-09T03:40:50-08:00","id":1193480,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyman201</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1193165#post1193165\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'd be surprised if they did that, honestly. It seems to run contrary to what they're trying to do with Scion. Trinity inspired a lot of Scion, yes, but Neall's been putting his own spin on things.<br /> <br /> Like the lack of Skill Tricks. Trinity has Skill Tricks, but in Scion there's <i>Knacks</i>.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but they're the unique setting bits getting layered over the Storypath core. It's hard to tell just now whether TC's use of specialties is a function of the setting, or part of Storypath, I wouldn't be surprised to see Specialties become more relevant either in Hero. Or they might not. It could just be that they stay as they are.<br /> <br /> Either way, I still think it's a change worth making. IMO, rules should incentivise the choices you want players to make. Storypath generally does. Except the weird free specialties. Because they incentivise going flat, setting every skill possible at 3, and then using Tweaks to actually build the character you want during play. The rules are saying &quot;Do it this way and get free points&quot;. Either, that's intended, and everyone should be encouraged to build their characters that way, or it's not intended, and the rules are working against the design intent.<br /> <br /> Even if they're not a lot of free points, it's still <i>objectively</i> the best way to build characters. The character who spreads their points out and then re-arranges later is <i>always</i> better off than one who doesn't - They're generating more Momentum and they have better access to Enhancements. It also exacerbates any group problems with meta-resources, because if some players do this and some don't, then you've got a situation where some players won't be contributing as much Momentum to the pool. <br /> <br /> I've been thinking and my current favourite solution is <br /> <br /> Every player starts with X* specialties. These must be assigned to Skills rated 3 or higher. If you have more Specialties available than skills at 3 or higher, they may be assigned to skills at 2 dots<br /> <br /> *I'd lean 4, personally, but any number between 3 and 7 fits within the spread you'd expect for the current system.<br /> <br /> This keeps experience parity between players. It resolves the bizarre incentivisation. It doesn't interact unfavourably with Tweaks. It still puts your specialties into the things you're actually good at.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2649-tarion","name":"Tarion","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"85"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-09T06:48:15-08:00","id":1193527,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyman201</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1192860#post1192860\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hey, sorry I got heated. I didn't intend it, but rereading I can see how they came across more aggressive than intended</div> </div> </div> </div>It's alright! I definitely found myself proofing posts a couple times near the end to make sure I wasn't getting aggressive myself. Internet forums can get wild :V <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23646-goblinsociety","name":"GoblinSociety","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-09T17:16:00-08:00","id":1193747,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Tarion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1193480#post1193480\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">*I'd lean 4, personally, but any number between 3 and 7 fits within the spread you'd expect for the current system.</div> </div> </div> </div>Slight problem with mixing up Trinity and Scion here. I haven't read all the previews for Trinity, but everything in the Origin text seems to indicate that Skills cap out at 5, at least at the mortal and probably Heroic tiers.<br /> <br /> Also, frankly, I think the problem I have with insisting that there will be a huge experience disparity is... Well... It has a few assumptions I don't quite agree with.<ul><li>One of the assumptions, that I mentioned upthread, is that it seems to assume people will gravitate towards getting a roughly equal number of Specialties. This seems like a mistaken assumption. It's not like, say, Exalted, where there's major mechanical benefits to having an extra dot of Dexterity or Stamina. It's not even an extra die like in Exalted.</li> <li>It seems to assume that there's One True Optimization which, again, seems like a flawed assumption. Not to keep banging on this drum, but people aren't going to use the same number of Specialties. Hell, the spread won't even be the same.<ul><li>To elaborate: If you're making a combat-specced character, you'll probably have a few Specialties, depending on your avenue. Maybe a Close Combat Specialty of [Weapon of Choice], possibly a Firearms Specialty if you like mixing it up...</li> <li>But if you're going to make say, a mental character, well then you could have an Occult Specialty, a Lore Specialty, a Technology Specialty... And you may get a Tech Specialty of Computers and one for Engineering, which <i>would</i> require buying at least one since you can't get two Free specialties in the same area.</li> <li>And a Social Spec may end up with say, multiple different Persuasion Specialties, and Integrity.</li> </ul></li> <li>Basically, people will likely get specialties in things they want to specialize <i>in</i>, rather than simply having a Specialty to have a number of Specialties.</li> </ul>And of course the <b><i>big</i></b> assumption is that Specialties seem to have far more mechanical impact than they're said to. Like your comment about how in Trinity Specialties are required for more finesse within a skill... And that may work for Trinity. But this isn't Trinity, it's Scion. There's no indication in the Origin book that Specialties will be required for Trinity-type granularity. Neall hasn't given any indication that I've seen for things like &quot;Can't craft anything without a Specialty&quot; or &quot;Can't pilot an unusual vehicle without a specialty&quot; being put into Scion, so I'm going to assume the Specialty rules in Origin are what we're going to get.<br /> <br /> Frankly, what do Specialties do?<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>They give Momentum when you fail in a roll it falls under. So if you have a Specialty in Swords, then failing to hit an enemy with a Sword attack will give the group +2 Momentum rather than +1</li> <li>They give +1 Enhancement to relevant rolls <i>outside</i> of the associated ability. So, say, if you make a Reason + Culture roll to identify a particular sword, you'd get +1 bonus success to realize <i>holy shit that's Gawain's sword</i>.</li> </ol>That's... Honestly about it. Specialties don't strike me as a huge problem for concern with experience disparity because frankly, <i>not</i> having as many Specialties as your buddy doesn't seem like the thing that will negatively effect your performance at the table. You'll be contributing slightly less Momentum, sure, but that's not quite as game-changing as a bonus success <i>to</i> the associated skill, or a bonus die.<br /> <br /> And frankly, if you min-max for Specialties, I think you may be the victim of unwise experience expenditure. Like, okay, let's just lay it out:<br /> <br /> Spending your Skill Dots for maximum Specialties will net you like, seven Specialties with seven Skills set to 3, which is what you need for Specialties. Let's be generous and say that the last two Skill Dots are spent raising two relevant skills up to 4, or one Skill from 3 to 5.<br /> <br /> A player who instead focuses on Skill Maximization will have like... Let's say four or so Skills set to 5, which will give them four Specialties. This also gives them 3 additional Skill dots, which let's say are put into a final Skill, for one more Specialty. So 5 total Specialties.<br /> <br /> Player B will need to buy three Specialties to match up to the three more than Player A got in Chargen, which is like 9 experience.<br /> <br /> Player A, however, if they want to up their Skills to 5 to match Player B, will need to buy six Skill dots to get four Skills from 3 to 5, to match Player B's focus. The Origin Book says that a new Skill Dot is <i><b>5</b></i> experience a pop, so they're spending like <i>3</i><i>0</i> experience.<br /> <br /> Of course this can be mitigated by swapping your skill dots around, but it seems to me that no matter how it maths out, it's far <i>less</i> effective to attribute your dots to acquire more Specialties than it is to buy up your Skills to <i>have higher Skills</i>.<br /> <br /> Now granted I'm coming from a person who comes up with Concept, then comes up with what the Paths will be for that character's life, and from <i>there </i>picks what Skills make sense to be associated with that Path rather than trying to game the numbers in any way. Storypath seems to be a laid-back enough system that mechanical optimization is difficult to pin down, and who's to say what 'Optimization' is?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-10T02:21:05-08:00","id":1193855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Kyman201</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1192205-specialties-after-character-generation?p=1193747#post1193747\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Slight problem with mixing up Trinity and Scion here. I haven't read all the previews for Trinity, but everything in the Origin text seems to indicate that Skills cap out at 5, at least at the mortal and probably Heroic tiers.</div> </div> </div> </div>The asterisk is attached to number of free specialties (The X in the line above), not the skill dot requirement. I'm saying to give out between 3 and 7 specialties for free, as the current system does. But just <i>do it</i> rather than different players getting different amounts.<br /> <br /> I also generally disagree with your view on specialties, I think. Mechanically, specialties in skills you're good at are less useful than in skills you're bad at. You want them supporting your core skills whenever possible, and you want them on skills you're more likely to fail.<br /> <br /> This is small scale, but let's imagine two takes on the same character. This guy is a Scion of Thor who grew up in near the Troll Preserves of Scandinavia. Because he's really buff, he actually <i>wrestles</i> these trolls. Partly to keep them off his livestock, partly because it's fun. One of the takes has Might 5, Close Combat at 5 and Occult 1 - He's better at wrestling trolls than talking about them. He's got a specialty in Wrestling.<br /> <br /> The other has Might 5, Close Combat 3 and Occult 3. Same dot expenditure as the first in everything else. His specialties are Close Combat (Wrestling) and Occult (Trolls). He is, right out of the gate, almost always better at wrestling trolls (And <i>always</i> better if he wants to shoot a troll, hunt a troll, hide from a troll, etc.). He's got the equivalent of 3 extra dice to it (1 free success whenever he succeeds in a roll). Whenever he fails an Occult roll (And the guy's probably got his mental stats as merely okay, so it'll happen a fair bit), he gains momentum. But, he may not always be encountering Trolls. In fact, after creating his character he realises he's probably going to be rolling Close Combat a hell of a lot more than Occult, so he makes sure he makes at least one Close Combat roll in the session and moves a dot to it. In the second session, he's Close Combat 4, Occult 2 and <i>still</i> getting a bonus to wrestle Trolls. And, by the start of the third session, he's Close Combat 5, Occult 1. He's identical to the first guy, except he's better in every situation in which he interacts with Trolls, and he gains bonuses whenever his (now) poor Occult skill fails him. At this point, life carries on, both versions put up the same skills, same knacks, same attributes. But, no matter what the first guy does, he's missing out on free dice and free momentum.<br /> <br /> The situation gets even worse when you start stacking up multiple specialties (Maybe he's got an Athletics specialty that can further complement his wrestling, but I'm blanking. I suppose a lifting related one might help when wrestling something bigger than a human. Maybe a Survival one related to Steve Irwining wildlife, because of the farming?). And, because it only takes two sessions to go from a 3/3 split to a 5/1 split, it's trivially easy to make the character like this, and always works out better. Even if you go full on specialty focused, 7 skills at 3, it's only six sessions to turn that into 3 at 5, 3 at 1, 1 at 3. Less than two months (For my game, anyway) to go from generalist to hyper-focused, and with a broad enough spread of those specialties you'll be getting regular Enhancements on your 5 dice pools and regular Momentum from your one dice ones.<br /> <br /> I <i>think</i> I've addressed all your points, but I don't want to turn this into one of those point-by-point quoting arguments, because they always spiral off into madness. The tl;dr is that having specialties supporting your main skills is <i>really</i> good. 1 Enhancement is huge. And having specialties in things you've got one dot in is equally good, because you'll fail them a lot. And because you can re-arrange your dots for free (and quickly), there's no reason not to.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2649,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2649-tarion","user":"Tarion","date":". 03-10-2018, 05:29 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2649-tarion","name":"Tarion","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"85"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-15T18:45:07-07:00","id":1195924,"title":"","content":"So, on the Scion Discord, Neall just clarified that you ARE in fact supposed to get a free Specialty if you buy your Skill up to 3 or higher post-Chargen. Hope this makes everyone happy","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Disclaimer: In favor of fun and enjoyment, but may speak up to warn you that you're gonna step on a metaphorical land mine","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2142-kyman201","name":"Kyman201","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2142_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4086"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}